module

public import Mathlib.FieldTheory.Perfect
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.AdicCompletion.Basic
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Algebraic.Defs
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.LocalRing.ResidueField.Basic
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Localization.FractionRing
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Noetherian.Defs
import WeilConjectures.RingTheory.CompleteLocalDomainFiniteOverRegular
import Mathlib.RingTheory.MvPowerSeries.Basic
import Mathlib.RingTheory.LaurentSeries

/-!
# Epp's field hypothesis for complete local domains

Source: Illusie--Laszlo--Orgogozo, Travaux de Gabber, Exposé IV, 3.2.1 and
Proposition 3.2.3(i),(iv), as applied in 4.2.3
(blueprint: lem-per-epp-perfect-core-complete-local).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace IsLocalRing

/-- If the perfect core of the residue field of a complete Noetherian local domain is
algebraic over a specified perfect subfield, so is the perfect core of its fraction field.
Membership in a perfect core is written as existence of a `p ^ n`-th root for every `n`.
Source: Illusie--Laszlo--Orgogozo, Travaux de Gabber, Exposé IV, 3.2.1 and
Proposition 3.2.3(i),(iv), as applied in 4.2.3.
(blueprint: lem-per-epp-perfect-core-complete-local)

Proof plan:
1. Apply `IsLocalRing.complete_local_domain_finite_over_regular`
   [lem-weil-pbc-complete-local-domain-finite-over-regular]. It gives a finite subring
   `A` with the same residue field, of the form `C[[t₁,...,t_d]]`: the alternative
   Cohen-ring case is impossible in characteristic `p`, since a prime integer is
   either zero or a unit and cannot generate a nonzero proper maximal ideal.
   By the constant-coefficient map and geometric-series inversion, the residue field
   of this power series ring is `C` [def-weil-pbc-multivariate-power-series]. Let
   `s : k → C ⊆ R` be the inverse residue isomorphism on constants.
2. This section preserves the given `k₀`. For `a ∈ k₀`, choose `a_n ∈ k₀` with
   `a_n ^ (p ^ n) = a`, using perfectness. Then
   `s(ā) - a = (s(ā_n) - a_n) ^ (p ^ n)` lies in the `p ^ n`-th power of the
   maximal ideal. These exponents are cofinal, and completeness includes separatedness
   [def-weil-pbc-adically-complete-ring], so `s(ā) = a`. The residue isomorphism
   commutes with all powers and identifies the perfect cores of `C` and `k`.
   The assumed algebraicity therefore holds for the perfect core of `C` over `k₀`.
3. Put `K = Frac(A)`. Localizing a finite `A`-module generating set of `R` gives a
   finite-dimensional domain over `K`. Multiplication by a nonzero element is an
   injective, hence surjective, endomorphism, so this domain is a field. It contains
   `R` and consists of fractions of elements of `R`, hence equals `F = Frac(R)`.
   Thus `F/K` is finite.
4. For any characteristic-`p` field `H`, the Laurent series ring `H((t))` is a field:
   invert a nonzero series `a t^r (1 + z)` by the geometric series for `1 + z`
   [def-weil-pbc-laurent-series]. Truncation and the characteristic-`p` binomial
   identity give `(Σ b_i t^i) ^ (p ^ n) = Σ b_i ^ (p ^ n) t^(i * p ^ n)`.
   An element having roots of all these orders has support only at exponent zero,
   since every support exponent is divisible by every `p ^ n`. Its constant has
   roots of every such order in `H`. Conversely such constants have constant roots.
   Therefore the perfect core of `H((t))` is precisely the constant perfect core of `H`.
5. Group multivariate coefficients successively to embed `A` in the iterated Laurent
   field `C((t₁))...((t_d))` [def-weil-pbc-multivariate-power-series]. The map is
   injective coefficientwise and respects multiplication, as each fixed multidegree
   has finitely many decompositions. It extends to `K` by fractions. Applying step 4
   repeatedly shows that the perfect core of `K` is that of `C`; the reverse inclusion
   uses constant roots. When `d = 0` this is the identity on `C`.
6. Apply [lem-per-finite-extension-perfect-core] to the finite extension `F/K`.
   Every element of the perfect core of `F` is algebraic over that of `K`, identified
   in step 5 with that of `C`. This includes inseparable finite extensions.
7. For such an element `x`, take a monic annihilating polynomial over the perfect core
   of `C`. Its finitely many coefficients are algebraic over `k₀` by step 2. The algebra
   generated by these coefficients and `x` is spanned by the finitely many monomials
   whose exponents are bounded by their monic equations. Linear dependence of enough
   powers of `x` gives a nonzero annihilating polynomial over `k₀`. Zero is covered by
   the polynomial `T`. This proves the required algebraicity.
-/
theorem epp_perfect_core_complete_local (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime]
    (k₀ : Type u) [Field k₀] [CharP k₀ p] [PerfectRing k₀ p]
    (R : Type v) [CommRing R] [IsLocalRing R] [IsNoetherianRing R] [IsDomain R]
    [CharP R p] [Algebra k₀ R] [IsAdicComplete (maximalIdeal R) R]
    (hres : ∀ x : ResidueField R,
      (∀ n : ℕ, ∃ y : ResidueField R, y ^ (p ^ n) = x) → IsAlgebraic k₀ x) :
    ∀ x : FractionRing R,
      (∀ n : ℕ, ∃ y : FractionRing R, y ^ (p ^ n) = x) → IsAlgebraic k₀ x := by
  sorry

end IsLocalRing
